---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Getting started in a working group"
  description="Membership, invitations, access, roles and a first-day checklist for SPDTF working-group participants."
>
  <PageMeta category="Working groups · Member guide" variant="brand" updated="Access guidance · 21 August 2026" />
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Participation begins with human approval and a recipient-specific invitation. A
    public expression of interest does not confer membership, Microsoft access, a vote
    or standards authority.
  </p>

  <h2 id="journey">From interest to participation</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Register through the public form or be nominated through an approved roster.</li>
    <li>OPDA reviews the person, organisation, intended group and appropriate sign-in identity.</li>
    <li>For an implemented workspace, OPDA provisions the guest identity and Team membership.</li>
    <li>Eligible company-domain participants receive access to their organisation's private source-intake folder.</li>
    <li>OPDA sends a recipient-specific invitation only after it has checked the access state.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The live Team roster is the participant authority for an implemented workspace.
    SharePoint permissions are derived from it; a folder or website page is not a
    separate membership register.
  </p>

  <h2 id="invitation">Use the account that was invited</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Open the invitation intended for you and sign in with the email account that received it.</li>
    <li>Do not forward or reuse the recipient-specific access link.</li>
    <li>If the identity is wrong, stop and ask OPDA to correct it instead of creating a competing account.</li>
    <li>Follow your organisation's security and device policies; do not bypass its controls.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="access-differences">Teams and SharePoint access differ</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Account situation</th><th>Teams discussion</th><th>SharePoint evidence intake</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Approved company-domain account</td><td>May be invited to the private Microsoft Team.</td><td>May receive the matching private organisation folder.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Approved generic-provider account</td><td>May be invited to discuss and review.</td><td>No provider-wide company folder is created.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expression of interest only</td><td>No access until human review and invitation.</td><td>No access.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A generic-provider account can participate in Teams but cannot define a safe
    organisation-wide SharePoint boundary. Arrange an approved company contributor if
    your organisation needs upload access.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-day">First-day checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Read General or Announcements for orientation and official notices.</li>
    <li>Review the channel directory and choose the most specific channel for a subject.</li>
    <li>Read the working group's scope, Evidence, Questions and Review pages on this site.</li>
    <li>Check existing threads before starting a new discussion.</li>
    <li>If contributing evidence, read the source-material rules before uploading anything.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="roles">What members own</h2>
  <p>
    Domain and scheme participants contribute evidence, explain language and rules,
    challenge examples and judge whether a candidate represents their work. Modelling
    facilitators make that meaning explicit and machine-readable. The Interoperability
    Working Group handles shared boundaries and mappings. The separate Technology
    Working Group supplies implementation and technical-assurance evidence; Property
    Technology remains one of the six property bounded contexts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="support">Access and administration support</h2>
  <p>
    For an additional colleague, an identity correction or a private access problem,
    email <a href="mailto:smartdata@openpropdata.org.uk">smartdata@openpropdata.org.uk</a>
    with the relevant group, names, email addresses and organisations. Email is an
    administration route, not a parallel group discussion list.
  </p>
</Layout>
